BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Newburyport Art presents May Exhibitions at 65 Water Street
DTSTART:20270127T110000
DTEND:20270127T170000
DTSTAMP:20260827T212256Z
UID:179da953738958c453ca3c25ec254f@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Newburyport Art (NAA) presents three new shows opening in the 
 month of May! Soft Structures is a group exhibition that explores fibers a
 nd fibrous material as process and metaphor. This exhibit features over 80
  artworks in a variety of media including drawing\, painting\, sculpture\,
  weaving\, embroidery\, quilting\, collage\, mixed media and more. This ex
 hibit is on view in the first-floor Sargent Gallery and second-floor Harts
 on Gallery. FOLIA is a collaborative exhibition featuring works by Amesbur
 y artists Amy Vander Els & Heather Karp. This exhibit explores the quiet b
 eauty and joyful rhythm of leaves and the symbolic meaning behind many tha
 t speaks of perseverance\, resilience and hope. Thirty-six artworks are di
 splayed amongst spring greenery in the first-floor Hills Gallery. Amy and 
 Heather will give a free artist talk on Thursday\, May 21st from 6:00-7:00
 pm\, concluding with Q&A. The 11th Annual Outdoor Sculpture Garden Exhibit
 ion debuted on May 1st\, and will remain on view year-round until April 20
 27. At nearly 3\,000 square feet\, the Range Lights Sculpture Garden resid
 es behind the historic NAA and is seen and visited by hundreds of people e
 very week. This year’s exhibition features 16 pieces by 10 local and reg
 ional artists. The participating artists include\; Jeffrey Briggs\, Lindle
 y Briggs\, Sarah Burns and Mike Platt\, Alexis Guieb-Alimbuyuguen\, Andrew
  Henschel\, Mae Kenny\, Evan Ralph\, Michael Updike and Raynor Warner. A r
 eception for FOLIA and Soft Structures will be held on Saturday\, May 2nd 
 from 4-6pm\, with the 11th Annual Sculpture Exhibit reception beginning at
  3:00pm\, including a Best in Show award at 3:30pm. The receptions are fre
 e and open to the public\, and visitors participating in the May 2nd Newbu
 ryport Artwalk are encouraged to stop by. New Hours: Wednesday through Sun
 day from 11:00am–5:00pm. The gallery is handicap accessible. More inform
 ation\, as well as an online gallery is available at newburyportart.org. F
 ounded in 1948\, Newburyport Art\, a 501(c)(3) non-profit\, is dedicated t
 o promoting and exhibiting members’ work and to providing art education 
 and access to the visual arts for the entire community. The gallery and ev
 ents are free and open to the public.\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent p
 age: https://northofboston.org/event/newburyport-art-presents-may-exhibiti
 ons-at-65-water-street/2027-01-27/
LOCATION:Newburyport Art Association\, 65 Water St\, Newburyport\, MA\, 01
 950
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/newburyport-art-presents-may-exhibitio
 ns-at-65-water-street/2027-01-27/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Fiona Brehony Explores Gloucester Harbor\, Whale Song\, and the Li
 ving Memory of Water
DTSTART:20270128T170000
DTEND:20270128T180000
DTSTAMP:20260827T212256Z
UID:4e7f1dba7054f8355539ee71e2135b@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:GOETEMANN ARTIST RESIDENCY BRINGS MANCHESTER-BASED ARTIST AND 
 WRITER TO GLOUCESTER OPENING TALK: THURSDAY SEPTEMBER 10 / (RNAC 5-6 PM) O
 PEN STUDIO: THURSDAY 2-4 PM 9/17 (OCEAN ALLIANCE) WALK/LISTEN: THURSDAY 9/
 24 2-4 PM (OCEAN ALLIANCE) CLOSING TALK: THURSDAY 10/1 4-5 PM (OCEAN ALLIA
 NCE) Gloucester\, MA – What can water remember? What stories are held wi
 thin a harbor\, a river\, or an ocean and whose stories are we able to hea
 r? These questions are at the heart of the work of artist and writer Fiona
  Brehony\, the current Goetemann Artist-in-Residence at Rocky Neck Art Col
 ony. Based in Manchester\, Britain\, Brehony works across moving image\, s
 ound\, and performance\, collaborating with scientists\, composers\, histo
 rians\, and communities to create poetic and inquisitive works that explor
 e the relationships between people\, places\, and waterways. During her re
 sidency in Gloucester\, Brehony is turning her attention to Gloucester Har
 bor\, exploring the harbor as both a natural ecosystem and a human-made in
 frastructure. Her work is informed by historian Richard White’s concept 
 of the “organic machine” – a landscape that is simultaneously natura
 l and shaped by human activity. This idea offers a way of approaching the 
 harbor not as a backdrop\, but as a complex living system in which water\,
  industry\, infrastructure\, human communities\, and other species are con
 tinually shaping one another. Brehony’s approach of thinking with water 
 considers water not simply as a subject or environment\, but as a material
  that connects bodies\, places\, histories\, and species. Drawing on Astri
 da Neimanis’s concept of Bodies of Water\, she is interested in the ways
  human bodies are already materially entangled with the waters around them
 . In Gloucester\, this raises questions about how we might listen to a har
 bor as bodies already implicated in its waters – and how whale song\, hu
 man voices\, harbor infrastructure\, and the movement of water might becom
 e part of a shared\, though uneven\, ecological and acoustic body. Her res
 idency includes a collaboration with Ocean Alliance\, engaging with the or
 ganization’s extensive archives of whale song to develop poetic work on 
 the ocean’s presence and the waters shared with whales. Brehony is explo
 ring the ocean as a living archive – one that carries ecological history
 \, community memory\, and evidence of the ways human and non-human lives i
 ntersect. “Water can hold histories that we don’t know how to access\,
 ” Brehony says. “I’m interested in what happens when we listen close
 ly to a place\, and in what might emerge when those acts of listening beco
 me collective.” The residency extends ideas developed through Brehony’
 s ongoing Liquid History project\, workshops and soundwalks approaching ri
 vers as living archives of social and ecological memory\, previously focus
 ed on the River Irk in North Manchester. In Gloucester\, that inquiry expa
 nds from river to harbor and ocean\, where the scale of water – and the 
 number of lives entangled within it – becomes harder to grasp. The publi
 c is invited to a series of gatherings and events during her time in Glouc
 ester. Brehony is an AHRC-funded doctoral candidate researching the River 
 Irk in North Manchester as a living site of cultural heritage and has spen
 t this summer as artist in residence with the Norwegian Institute for Natu
 re Research in Oslo\, developing Water Symphony\, following the city’s f
 jord\, rivers\, and waterways as a medium for exploring urban justice. Her
  work has been commissioned by the Whitworth Art Gallery\, the University 
 of Oxford\, PRiSM at the Royal Northern College of Music\, and Creswell Cr
 ags Museum\, and supported by Arts Council England and the AHRC. The Goete
 mann Artist Residency Program is a program of Rocky Neck Art Colony\, brin
 ging contemporary artists to Gloucester to engage with the community\, exp
 lore the region’s rich cultural and environmental landscape\, and develo
 p new work in response to place. For more information about Fiona Brehony
 ’s residency\, upcoming public programs\, and the Goetemann Artist Resid
 ency Program\, visit Rocky Neck Art Colony at rockyneckartcolony.org.\n\nS
 ources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https://northofboston.org/event/fiona
 -brehony-explores-gloucester-harbor-whale-song-and-the-living-memory-of-wa
 ter/2027-01-28/
LOCATION:Rocky Neck
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/fiona-brehony-explores-gloucester-harb
 or-whale-song-and-the-living-memory-of-water/2027-01-28/
END:VEVENT
